<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_where.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-where">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-where">
  <primary>WHERE</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>WHERE</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>WHERE</refname>
  <refpurpose>constrain a pattern or filter a projection</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
WHERE <replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable>
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-where-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <literal>WHERE</literal> drops every row whose condition is not true. It is not a clause of
   its own. It attaches to one of three clauses and takes its meaning from the one it attaches
   to.
  </para>

  <para>
   On <xref linkend="cypher-match"/> it is part of the match. Every variable of the pattern is
   bound when the condition runs, and a match that fails it is not produced.
  </para>

  <para>
   On <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal> it is part of the optional match. A row that fails the
   condition is kept, with the variables of the pattern set to null.
  </para>

  <para>
   On <xref linkend="cypher-with"/> it filters the projected rows, after any grouping. This is
   where a condition over an aggregate goes.
  </para>

  <para>
   The condition has to be a boolean expression. A value of another type is not coerced: a
   jsonb string or number in a condition raises an error rather than counting as true.
   Aggregates are not allowed in a <literal>WHERE</literal>. Put the aggregate in a
   <command>WITH</command> and filter its output.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>WHERE</literal> on its own is a syntax error, and a query cannot end with it. The
   standalone filter that can stand anywhere in a query is <xref linkend="cypher-filter"/>,
   which accepts both <literal>FILTER condition</literal> and
   <literal>FILTER WHERE condition</literal>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-where-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      An expression of type <type>boolean</type> over the variables in scope. Combine tests
      with <literal>AND</literal>, <literal>OR</literal>, <literal>XOR</literal> and
      <literal>NOT</literal>. See <xref linkend="cypher-expr"/>.
     </para>
     <para>
      A value of another type is refused rather than treated as true.
      <literal>WHERE m.title</literal> raises
      <literal>cannot cast "The Matrix" (jsonb type string) to boolean</literal>. Compare
      explicitly, or use <literal>IS NOT NULL</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-where-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Compare a property:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.year &gt; 2015
RETURN m.title AS film, m.year AS year
ORDER BY year, film;

            film            | year
----------------------------+------
 "Blade Runner 2049"        | 2017
 "The Matrix Resurrections" | 2021
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Combine conditions:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.genre = 'Action' AND m.year &lt; 1995
RETURN m.title AS film
ORDER BY film;

             film
------------------------------
 "Terminator 2: Judgment Day"
 "The Terminator"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>XOR</literal> is available alongside <literal>AND</literal>,
   <literal>OR</literal> and <literal>NOT</literal>:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.genre = 'Action' XOR m.year &lt; 1995
RETURN count(*) AS n;

 n
---
 8
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>label()</literal> returns the label of an element as a string, for vertices and
   edges alike. <literal>IN</literal> over a list of names is how one pattern matches several
   edge labels, which an edge pattern cannot express:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[r]-&gt;(m:movie)
WHERE label(r) IN ['acted_in', 'directed']
RETURN count(r) AS edges;

 edges
-------
 46
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>STARTS WITH</literal>, <literal>ENDS WITH</literal> and
   <literal>CONTAINS</literal> test a string property, and <literal>=~</literal> matches a
   POSIX regular expression. Negate any of them with <literal>NOT</literal>; there is no
   <literal>!~</literal> for a property:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.title =~ '^Blade.*'
RETURN m.title AS film
ORDER BY film;

        film
---------------------
 "Blade Runner"
 "Blade Runner 2049"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A property that was never set reads as null, so <literal>IS NULL</literal> is the test for
   its absence. No film carries a <literal>runtime</literal>, so all eighteen match:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.runtime IS NULL
RETURN count(*) AS n;

 n
----
 18
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A subquery value expression tests whether a pattern exists without binding anything from
   it:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person) WHERE EXISTS { MATCH (p)-[:directed]-&gt;() }
RETURN p.name AS director
ORDER BY director
LIMIT 4;

      director
---------------------
 "Andrew Niccol"
 "Christopher Nolan"
 "Denis Villeneuve"
 "James Cameron"
(4 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   An aggregate in a <literal>WHERE</literal> is refused:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie) WHERE count(m) &gt; 1
RETURN p.name;

ERROR:  aggregate functions are not allowed in WHERE
LINE 1: MATCH (p:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie) WHERE count(m) &gt; 1
                                                      ^
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Aggregate in a <command>WITH</command> and filter the result instead. The
   <literal>WHERE</literal> of a <command>WITH</command> runs after the grouping:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie)
WITH p, count(m) AS films WHERE films &gt;= 3
RETURN p.name AS actor, films
ORDER BY actor;

          actor          | films
-------------------------+-------
 "Arnold Schwarzenegger" | 3
 "Keanu Reeves"          | 4
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-where-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   A property holds a jsonb value, and comparing it to a literal compares jsonb values. In
   jsonb ordering a number sorts above every string, so a quoted number compared against a
   numeric property is not the comparison you meant:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
RETURN m.year &gt; '9999' AS num_gt_str, m.year &gt; 1990 AS num_gt_num;

 num_gt_str | num_gt_num
------------+------------
 t          | t
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Write the literal in the type of the property, or cast the property with
   <literal>m.year::int &gt; 1990</literal>, when the stored type is not the one you expect.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-where-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 attaches a search condition to a graph pattern in the same place, so
   <literal>MATCH ... WHERE</literal> conforms. The labeled predicate of section 19.9, written
   <literal>n IS LABELED movie</literal> or as the shorthand <literal>n:movie</literal>, is not
   accepted; write <literal>label(n) = 'movie'</literal>. The existential subquery of section
   19.4 is accepted, as <literal>EXISTS { MATCH ... }</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher attaches <literal>WHERE</literal> to <command>MATCH</command>,
   <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal> and <command>WITH</command> the same way. A bare pattern
   predicate is not an expression here, so <literal>WHERE (m)-[:related_to]-&gt;(:movie)</literal>
   is a syntax error and so is the unlabelled <literal>WHERE (m)-[]-&gt;()</literal>. Wrap it in
   <literal>EXISTS { MATCH ... }</literal>. SQL's <literal>LIKE</literal> and
   <literal>NOT LIKE</literal> are not part of a Cypher condition either, with or without a
   cast. Use <literal>STARTS WITH</literal>, <literal>ENDS WITH</literal>,
   <literal>CONTAINS</literal> or <literal>=~</literal>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

